            <section id="editor" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">The editor</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">One editor for creating and changing components. There are two ways to work. <strong>Add Component</strong> (top bar) or <strong>New component</strong> (Library) opens a quick dialog: write or paste code on the left, see the result on the right, press <strong>Continue</strong> to name it and publish. For visual editing press <strong>Full editor</strong> in that dialog (or the expand icon above the preview): your code comes with you into the design canvas. To change an existing component, press <strong>Edit</strong> on its card in your <a href="/library" class="font-medium underline">Library</a>; that opens the canvas directly. The canvas works like Figma: click things, change them, see the result instantly. Its <strong>Back</strong> link returns to where you came from.</p>

                <svg viewBox="0 0 760 130" class="mt-6 w-full rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white" role="img" aria-label="Start, edit, save">
                    <defs><marker id="a2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" fill="#111"/></marker></defs>
                    <rect x="20" y="30" width="150" height="70" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#d1d5db"/><text x="95" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#111">1. Start</text><text x="95" y="82" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6b7280">AI, paste, or a block</text>
                    <rect x="210" y="30" width="150" height="70" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#d1d5db"/><text x="285" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#111">2. Edit</text><text x="285" y="82" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6b7280">click and change</text>
                    <rect x="400" y="30" width="150" height="70" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#d1d5db"/><text x="475" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#111">3. Name it</text><text x="475" y="82" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6b7280">title, tags, visibility</text>
                    <rect x="590" y="30" width="150" height="70" rx="10" fill="#000"/><text x="665" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#fff">Save</text><text x="665" y="82" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#d1d5db">into your Library</text>
                    <path d="M170 65 H208" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#a2)"/><path d="M360 65 H398" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#a2)"/><path d="M550 65 H588" stroke="#111" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#a2)"/>
                </svg>

                <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Starting a new component</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm">An empty canvas shows three ways to begin:</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
                    <li><strong>Generate with AI.</strong> Describe it ("a pricing section with three tiers"). The AI looks at your library so the result matches your style. Needs AI turned on (avatar menu, <em>AI Settings</em>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Paste HTML or code.</strong> Opens the <strong>Code</strong> panel. Paste HTML with Tailwind, CSS (<code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">&lt;style&gt;</code>), JavaScript (<code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">&lt;script&gt;</code>), or a whole page. The canvas updates as you type.</li>
                    <li><strong>Start with a section.</strong> Drops in a simple block, then use <strong>+ Add</strong> to build: sections, columns, headings, text, buttons, images, cards and inputs.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Editing</h3>
                <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-4"><p class="font-medium text-gray-900">Floating toolbar</p><p class="mt-1 text-sm">Select something and a small toolbar appears next to it. On text: font, size, color, <strong>B</strong> <em>I</em> <u>U</u>, link and alignment (while you type it formats just the selected words). On anything else: fill, text color and corner radius. The <strong>&#8942;</strong> menu has duplicate, reorder, free position, group and delete.</p></div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-4"><p class="font-medium text-gray-900">Canvas</p><p class="mt-1 text-sm">Click anything to select it, double-click text to type on it. Two floating bars hold everything else: <strong>designs and tools</strong> on the left, <strong>undo, code, preview, panel and zoom</strong> on the right.</p></div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-4"><p class="font-medium text-gray-900">Panel (closed by default)</p><p class="mt-1 text-sm">Open it with the panel button for every property. <strong>Properties</strong> has three tabs: <strong>Design</strong>: layout, flex and grid, size, a spacing box, position, typography, fill and gradients, borders and radius, shadows, filters, transforms and transitions. <strong>Classes and CSS</strong>: every class as a chip, custom CSS and attributes. <strong>AI</strong>: change the selected element by asking.</p></div>
                </div>
                <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
                    <li><strong>Tailwind or CSS, automatically.</strong> At the top of the Design tab, <em>Auto</em> picks the right panel for each element. Elements styled with Tailwind classes get the Tailwind controls. Elements styled by the design's own CSS (a stylesheet, Bootstrap, a Claude Design export) get a <strong>CSS</strong> panel that shows their real current values and saves your changes as inline styles, which override the stylesheet without touching it. Switch by hand with <em>Tailwind</em> or <em>CSS</em> any time. A blue bar marks values you set, and the x button removes one so the element falls back to the stylesheet. For hover or breakpoint styles use Tailwind mode, or edit the stylesheet in the <strong>Code</strong> panel.</li>
                    <li><strong>Breakpoints and states.</strong> At the top of the Design tab, pick <em>md and up</em>, <em>hover</em>, <em>dark</em> and so on. Every control then edits that variant (for example <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">md:hover:bg-blue-700</code>). Choosing a breakpoint also resizes the canvas to match.</li>
                    <li><strong>Any value.</strong> Fields suggest Tailwind values but accept anything: type <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">50%</code>, <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">24px</code> or <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">#ff0000</code> and it becomes an arbitrary class like <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">w-[50%]</code>. Color boxes have a full palette, a custom picker and opacity. Anything Tailwind lacks can be added as custom CSS.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ask AI</strong> (AI tab): describe a change to just the selected element, for example "make this a dark card".</li>
                    <li><strong>Up, Down, Duplicate, Delete</strong> work on the selected element. Shortcuts: <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd+Z</kbd> undo, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Shift+Z</kbd> redo, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd+D</kbd> duplicate, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Delete</kbd>, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd+S</kbd> save.</li>
                    <li><strong>Code</strong> shows the full source and stays in sync both ways. <strong>Edit / Preview:</strong> Preview runs the component with its JavaScript so you can try menus and buttons.</li>
                    <li>Your component's own CSS and scripts are kept exactly as they were. Only the markup you edit changes.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Handles</h3>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
                    <li><strong>Corners:</strong> drag a corner square to resize. Move the pointer just <em>outside</em> a corner and the cursor becomes a curved arrow: drag to rotate (<kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Shift</kbd> snaps to 15 degrees). The selection box turns with the element.</li>
                    <li><strong>Edges:</strong> drag anywhere along an edge to change just the width or height. Every element has all four corners and edges, including ones that sit in the normal page flow (the opposite side stays where it is). <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Shift</kbd> keeps the proportions and <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Alt</kbd> resizes from the center.</li>
                    <li><strong>Corner dots:</strong> the small round dot just inside each corner sets the corner radius. Drag one toward the middle and all four corners round together (the number is also in the floating toolbar). It is saved as a Tailwind class, or an inline style on non-Tailwind designs.</li>
                    <li><strong>A whole frame:</strong> click a frame's name to select it. Its handles resize the frame and the design inside reflows to fit. The bar below it has <em>Desktop</em>, <em>Tablet</em> and <em>Mobile</em> presets and exact <em>W</em> and <em>H</em> (leave H empty to grow with the content). The size is saved with the design.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">One infinite plane, many designs</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm">The canvas is an endless plane, like Claude Design or Figma. Every design in the same <a href="#projects" class="font-medium underline">project</a> sits on it side by side, so you can see the whole product at once and jump between screens without going back to the Library.</p>
                <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
                    <li><strong>One live frame.</strong> The design with the blue name is the one you are editing. The others are live previews. <strong>Click a preview</strong> (or pick it from the <strong>designs menu</strong> at the top left) to switch to it. Each design keeps its own undo history, so switching never loses an edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pan and zoom.</strong> Scroll to pan, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd</kbd> + scroll or a trackpad pinch to zoom around the pointer, drag the background or hold <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Space</kbd> to pan. The zoom menu has <em>Fit all</em> (<kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Shift+1</kbd>) and <em>Fit this design</em> (<kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Shift+2</kbd>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Arrange.</strong> Drag a frame by its name to move it. Positions are saved, and the project board shows the same layout.</li>
                    <li><strong>Add a design.</strong> <strong>+ New design</strong> in that menu adds an empty frame to the plane (it asks you to name a project first if this design isn't in one). It is saved into the project as its own page.</li>
                    <li><strong>Save</strong> writes every design you changed, each as a new version. A blue dot next to a name means it has unsaved changes.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Free design tools</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm">The floating palette at the top of the canvas works like Figma. Pick a tool (or press its key) and drag on the canvas to draw. Everything you draw is real HTML, positioned with <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 font-mono text-[13px]">position: absolute</code>, so the <strong>Code</strong> panel always shows exactly what you have designed.</p>
                <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white">
                    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                        <thead class="bg-gray-50 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500"><tr><th class="px-4 py-2.5">Tool</th><th class="px-4 py-2.5">Key</th><th class="px-4 py-2.5">What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-gray-900">Move</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5"><kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">V</kbd></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Select, drag, resize, rotate. Drag on empty space to select several things.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-gray-900">Hand</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5"><kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">H</kbd> or hold <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Space</kbd></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Pan around the canvas.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-gray-900">Frame</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5"><kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">F</kbd></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">A container box. Draw things inside it and they belong to it.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-gray-900">Rectangle, Ellipse</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5"><kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">R</kbd> <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">O</kbd></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Drag to draw a shape. Change color, corners and shadow in the inspector.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-gray-900">Text</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5"><kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">T</kbd></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Click and type. Double-click any text later to edit it.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-gray-900">Image, Button</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5"><kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">I</kbd> <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">B</kbd></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Draw a placeholder image or a button. You can also drop an image file straight onto the canvas.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
                    <li><strong>Move and resize:</strong> drag a shape, or its handles. Smart red guides snap to other shapes and to the edges and center of what contains it (hold <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Alt</kbd> to turn snapping off). <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Shift</kbd> keeps the proportions or the straight line. The round handle above a shape rotates it (Shift snaps to 15 degrees).</li>
                    <li><strong>Arrow keys</strong> nudge by 1 px, with Shift by 10 px. Exact X, Y, width, height and rotation are in <strong>Position and size</strong> in the inspector.</li>
                    <li><strong>Several things at once:</strong> Shift-click or drag a box around them. Then <strong>Align</strong> (left, center, right, top, middle, bottom), <strong>space them evenly</strong>, or <strong>Group</strong> them (<kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd+G</kbd>; Ungroup is <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+G</kbd>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Free position</strong> turns any existing element (a heading from AI, for example) into one you can drag anywhere. Without it, elements stay in the page's normal flow and dragging reorders them.</li>
                    <li><strong>Shortcuts:</strong> <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Ctrl/Cmd+D</kbd> duplicates, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Delete</kbd> removes, <kbd class="rounded border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-1.5 text-xs">Esc</kbd> goes back to Move.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Saving</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm">For a new component, <strong>Save</strong> asks for the details: <strong>Title</strong> and <strong>Description</strong> (what a person or an AI reads to decide if it fits), <strong>Category</strong>, <strong>Tags</strong> (comma separated, used for search and by AI) and <strong>Visibility</strong> (<em>Public</em> shows it in the gallery too, <em>Private</em> keeps it in your library; see <a href="#visibility" class="font-medium underline">Public or private</a>). After that, <strong>Save</strong> makes a new version each time. Click the title at the top to change the details later.</p>
            </section>

